  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 254 AM CDT Fri Sep 18 2026\n\nA slow moving trough currently over eastern Oregon will very\nslowly push east this weekend, eventually nudging the ridge (and\nthe accompanying heat) south. The trough emerges out into the\nNorthern Plains on Sunday with a cold front sagging southward\nthrough Iowa. This is a heat ending cold front, but the heat\nwill already be waning ahead of it. The ridge will be flattening\nout and temperatures on the warm side of the front will top out\na few degrees cooler than Saturday, only in the low to mid 90s.\nWith dewpoints continued modest, in the 60s, the likelihood of\nanother day of 100+ heat index values is lower. Thus we'll keep\nthe Heat Advisory ending on Saturday for now. The front\ngradually pushes southward across the forecast area Sunday into\nMonday with daytime highs maxing out in the 70s north of the\nfront compared to the 90s ahead of it. There's more point based\nuncertainty on the temperature forecast during this time period\ndue to timing uncertainty with that front. Subtle shortwave\ntroughs tracking east ahead of the main wave will cause subtle\nmovements in the surface front as well. Bursts of convection \nalong and behind it could also help to push the front southward.\n\nShower and thunderstorm chances increase along and behind the\nfront as it makes its way slowly southward. The timing of each\nround will be influenced by those subtle waves tracking along it\nand the corresponding moisture fluxes near the boundary. We\ndon't expect it to be a nonstop rain, but it will be an overall\nwetter period. While the timing and location uncertainty limits\nthe current precipitation chances to 70 percent or so in any one\nforecast period, confidence is higher that just about the entire\nregion will get rain at some point as the front moves through,\nwith the best chances Sunday and Monday. For example, among the\nlong range ensemble guidance, nearly 100 percent of members\nproduce measurable rainfall in the 24 hours ending Monday\nafternoon indicating that when it's all said and done just about\neveryone should have gotten wet. The average QPF from the same\nensemble group is a modest 0.3 to 0.5 inches, though some\nlocally higher amounts over 1 inch are possible where \nthunderstorms linger.\n\nLong range guidance is in generally good agreement that the\ntrough shifts east and the front pushes far enough south that\nprecipitation chances end by Tuesday morning. While the coolest\nair takes aim further east toward New England, we still do get a\nnoticeably cool push locally as well. Ensemble mean 925mb \ntemperatures around +15C translates to about 70 degrees in a \nfully mixed environment. If clouds are slow to clear then it may\nbe even cooler than that. The surface high bringing this air\nmass lingers over Wisconsin for several days, keeping winds\nlocally out of the north or northeast for several days. While\nthe air mass will gradually modify over time, we don't see a\nreturn of the heat and humidity of recent weeks.",
